Based on the discussion, this appears to be a walkthrough of a patient escalation identification process that currently uses SQL/Alteryx workflows to identify patients at risk of escalation, followed by a conversation about automating the process and eventually enhancing it with AI.
Understanding Document
Business Purpose
The current process identifies patients who are most likely to require intervention before they escalate into service issues. The goal is to proactively surface high-risk patients to contact center representatives so that issues can be resolved before becoming formal escalations.
Currently, the process:
•	Pulls inbound contact center call data.
•	Combines multiple patient, task, prescription, and escalation data sources.
•	Applies business rules and categorization logic.
•	Produces a focused work queue of approximately 200-400 patients per day.
•	Requires manual execution and Excel-based processing.
The future goal is to:
1.	Automate the process end-to-end.
2.	Feed the results directly into Pega.
3.	Expand patient coverage using AI-driven analysis.
 
Current Data Sources
1. Contact Center Call Data
Included:
•	Inbound calls only
•	Calls associated with specific Contact Center Directors
•	Calls with valid Patient IDs
Excluded:
•	Physician call data
•	Outbound calls
•	AOM calls
•	Calls without Patient IDs
Reason
Patients without IDs cannot be connected to other enterprise systems and therefore cannot be analyzed effectively.
 
2. Patient Information
Additional patient data is brought in, including:
•	Patient Name
•	Patient ID
•	Date of Birth
•	Demographic information
•	Patient account information
 
3. Duplicate Account Detection
The process identifies duplicate patient accounts using:
•	Same patient name
•	Same date of birth
•	Different patient IDs
Business Problem
Duplicate accounts may cause:
•	Prior Authorizations (PAs) entered under one account
•	Approvals entered under another account
•	Delays in patient processing
•	Confusion among agents
Patients with duplicate accounts are categorized separately for easier resolution.
 
4. Task Data
Various patient tasks are pulled into the process.
Task information is used to identify:
•	Open work items
•	Delayed processing
•	Potential shipment issues
 
5. Existing Operational Intelligence Data
The query references an Ops Intel/Ops Insight table.
Purpose:
•	Exclude patients that are already being monitored
•	Prevent duplicate records
•	Avoid duplicate work queues
 
Processing Method
Temporary Tables
All source datasets are first loaded into temporary tables.
Benefits:
•	Improves query performance
•	Simplifies final joins
•	Reduces processing time
 
Final Consolidated Query
A master query joins:
•	Call data
•	Patient data
•	Prescription data
•	Task data
•	Escalation history
•	Duplicate account flags
This query applies multiple categorization rules.
 
Patient Categorization Logic
1. Priority Therapy
Patients are categorized as Priority Therapy when they belong to predefined high-priority therapies.
Purpose:
•	Allow teams to focus on critical therapies first.
 
2. Duplicate Accounts
Patients identified by the duplicate account logic are categorized accordingly.
Purpose:
•	Alert representatives to account consolidation issues.
 
3. Previous Escalations
Patients are categorized if:
•	They had an escalation within the previous 6 months
•	But NOT within the last 14 days
Rationale
Patients with recent escalations are already being managed.
Patients with historical escalations:
•	Have a higher likelihood of escalating again
•	Are valuable targets for proactive intervention
 
4. CCC Limitations
Patients are categorized as CCC Limitation when:
•	Specific status codes are present
•	Task creation date exceeds 10 days
Purpose:
•	Identify known operational issues requiring attention.
 
5. Other
Any remaining patients are categorized as:
•	Other
 
Escalation Exclusion Logic
Patients are excluded when:
Recent Escalation
Escalation occurred within:
•	Last 14 days
Reason:
•	Already being actively worked by escalation teams.
 
Repeat Caller Logic
Patients are included when:
•	They called within the last 14 days
•	Have not formally escalated
Reason:
These patients may:
•	Be repeat callers
•	Be experiencing unresolved issues
•	Represent future escalation risks
 
Prescription and Shipment Rules
Patients are flagged when:
•	Tasks exist within the prescription processing window
•	Orders have not shipped
•	HID (Home Infusion Delivery) date is approaching
Specific rules include:
Next Delivery Date
Look ahead:
•	7 days
Purpose:
Identify patients requiring deliveries soon.
 
Missing Shipment
Flag when:
•	HID date approaching
•	No shipment date recorded
 
Missing Profile Lock
Flag when:
•	Profile lock date is absent
 
Open Tasks
Only consider:
•	Incomplete tasks
Exclude:
•	Completed tasks
 
Call Activity Requirement
The final dataset only includes:
Patients who have contacted the contact center within the previous 14 days.
Reason:
The process is intended to identify active patients experiencing potential service issues.
 
Current Output
Daily patient volume:
Time Period	Volume
Beginning of Week	300-400
Later in Week	<200
Typical Average	~200
The current logic intentionally narrows the patient population to reduce false positives and keep workloads manageable.
 
Current Manual Process
Today the workflow requires:
1.	Run Alteryx package
2.	Generate patient list
3.	Export results to Excel
4.	Compare against master spreadsheet
5.	Remove accounts already worked within previous 28 days
6.	Publish final list for users
Limitation
The team lacks write access to a database or ETL platform.
Because of this:
•	Excel is used as temporary storage
•	Deduplication is performed manually
 
Future State (Phase 1)
Automation
Automate the existing workflow by:
•	Scheduling SQL jobs
•	Reading data directly from Databricks/Oracle
•	Running business logic automatically
•	Loading results directly into Pega
Benefits:
•	Eliminate manual execution
•	Remove Excel dependency
•	Create a single source of truth for users
 
Future State (Phase 2)
AI Agent Expansion
Current process reviews:
•	Approximately 200 patients daily
Future AI-enabled process could:
•	Evaluate 1,000-2,000+ patients daily
•	Relax current filtering constraints
•	Detect escalation risk in broader populations
•	Reduce dependency on manual review capacity
The AI agent would start with the current rules and eventually expand beyond the current patient criteria.
 
Executive Summary
The current solution is a rules-based escalation prevention workflow that combines call data, patient information, prescription status, tasks, and historical escalations to identify patients likely to require intervention. The process intentionally produces a small, highly targeted patient population (~200/day) to minimize false positives. Execution is currently manual and dependent on Alteryx and Excel.
The proposed enhancement is a two-phase modernization effort:
1.	Automate the existing query and feed results directly into Pega through an ETL process.
2.	Introduce AI-driven patient analysis to expand the population reviewed and proactively identify additional escalation risks beyond the current rule-based framework.
 
